homelab-search (0.3.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A renamed or deleted mail folder no longer lingers in the index
    forever. search.mailboxes.name IS the on-disk folder name, UNIQUE
    (account_id, name), and mailbox_id() is get-or-create -- so renaming
    '.Old' to '.New' never renamed anything here: it created a SECOND row
    and re-indexed every message under fresh ids, while '.Old' and all its
    messages stayed searchable, pointing at a folder that was no longer
    there. Nothing in this package had ever removed a mailbox row; the
    drive side has had a reconcile from the start and mail had none.
  * New Index::prune_mailboxes, called from _discover_mail at the one
    point where the folder list is trustworthy: after the account
    directory has been read SUCCESSFULLY (a failed read does `next` well
    above it) and with @boxes complete and always containing at least
    INBOX.
  * ONE DELETE, because every child cascades -- messages and
    message_files hang off (account_id, mailbox_id) ON DELETE CASCADE, and
    bodies, addresses, headers and attachments cascade from messages.
    Staging it by hand would be more code and more ways to leave half of
    it behind.
  * Two guards, because the cost of being wrong is somebody's mail
    vanishing from search: an empty or missing folder list deletes nothing
    and does not even open a transaction (every account has at least
    INBOX, so an empty list means the CALLER failed, not that the account
    has no folders), and the delete is scoped to a single account_id so
    even a wrong list cannot reach past the account being reconciled.
  * Verified against production read-only before shipping: across every
    account and every folder, the number of mailboxes this would have
    pruned is ZERO, and no account directory was unreadable. It deletes
    nothing today and exists for the first rename.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 21:41:30 -0600

homelab-search (0.3.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * An unreadable message directory no longer wipes a mailbox out of the
    index. A mailbox is two directories -- `cur` holding read mail, which
    is nearly all of it, and `new` holding the unread. _do_mailbox listed
    both into one list and then deleted from the index anything it had not
    seen. Three conditions skipped a directory (an unsafe path, a failed
    stat, a failed opendir) and all three fell through to that deletion,
    so an unreadable `cur` looked exactly like an empty one: every read
    message was removed and the unit was recorded as a SUCCESS, with the
    watermark advanced so discovery would not come back until that folder
    changed again.
  * No mail was ever at risk -- the store is mounted read-only, with the
    server-side export read-only too -- but it disappeared from search.
    The trigger is a momentary ESTALE or EACCES on the NAS, which is
    routine, and likelier still because 5 of 26 account directories are
    0700 vmail:vmail with manage-gids on the NAS.
  * Now: a directory that exists and cannot be read fails the unit, which
    run_unit's existing eval routes to _fail -- error recorded, attempt
    counted, retried, parked after five. Nothing is deleted and the
    watermark does not advance. A partial view is not evidence of
    deletion; it is evidence of nothing.
  * ABSENT and UNREADABLE are now distinguished, which is what the fix
    turns on: plenty of mailboxes have only one of the two directories,
    and failing those would have parked every single one.
  * t/indexer.t -- the indexer's first tests, and it was the only major
    module in this package with none, which is what the above cost. A
    scratch maildir in a temp directory, no database and no NFS, fixtures
    built in the test so the repository carries no binaries. The bug's
    test was written first and failed before the fix. Also pins three
    behaviours that were correct and undefended: an unchanged re-scan
    writes nothing, a flag change is a one-column update rather than a
    re-index (getting that wrong re-indexes a mailbox every time someone
    reads their mail), and a genuinely deleted message is still removed.
  * A database blip no longer 500s the search page. _ai_state runs on
    every signed-in render and opens a transaction -- Consent::state_for
    writes the notice row when absent -- so it needed a live database even
    when nobody had asked for AI mode, outside the error handling that
    exists to make this page degrade. A failure there now only hides the
    AI offer; the keyword results, which are what the page is for, render
    regardless.
  * Removed a dead variable whose name contained Cyrillic homoglyphs
    (`$hидden`) in a file with no `use utf8`.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 21:27:25 -0600

homelab-search (0.3.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * One search page asked "what is shared with me?" three times -- once
    for the results, once for the counts, once for the file-type facets
    -- rebuilding the whole list each time. With a 6,934-file share that
    was ~75ms of a 116ms render spent re-answering a question already
    answered microseconds earlier. Now resolved once per request.
  * Deliberately NOT cached across requests, though the share
    fingerprint is cheap and would catch both a grant and a revoke.
    Three other things change what is shared with you while touching no
    row in drive.shares: a share passing its expires_at, a file added to
    a shared folder, and a file trashed inside one. A fingerprint-keyed
    cache would serve a confidently stale allowlist. A request is the
    widest window in which nothing is expected to change.
  * The share fingerprint gained a fourth number, and this one is a
    correctness fix rather than a speed one. The first three move when
    somebody DOES something -- grant, revoke. A share passing its
    expires_at is nobody doing anything, so the fingerprint sat still
    while the share stopped being live, and an AI answer describing its
    files stayed cached for the rest of the TTL. The live search path
    always got expiry right; only the cache could lag. The fourth number
    counts the shares live at read time, so an expiry moves it.
    Migration 015 moves the column default to match, which retires the
    answers computed under the three-number contract.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 19:27:46 -0600

homelab-search (0.3.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * AI search no longer dies at 40 seconds. The client set
    request_timeout(120) but left inactivity_timeout at Mojo's 40-second
    default, so the 120-second ceiling was never 120 seconds: a
    non-streaming generation that thinks for 40 seconds without sending a
    byte was killed well short of it. A grantee searching across
    thousands of shared files is exactly the question slow enough to
    reach that, and it did.
  * ...and when it died it died loudly. Mojo's ->result CROAKS on a
    transport error rather than returning one, so the timeout escaped
    `call` as an exception, past every ok=>0 branch written to turn a
    failed call into a message for the person asking. Both faults
    pre-date shares; the shared-file pass is just the first question slow
    enough to find them.

