homelab-search (0.3.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Shared-file resolution actually returns its rows. The subtree query
    wrote `@{ $db->query(...)->hashes->@* }`, which dereferences twice:
    the postfix ->@* already flattens the collection to a list, and
    wrapping that in @{ BLOCK } evaluates the block in scalar context, so
    the block yielded the row count and Perl refused to use that integer
    as a reference -- "Can't use string (6934) as an ARRAY ref".
  * Why 442 tests did not catch it: until the GRANT on drive.shares was
    applied, shared_files returned early for every account, so the
    subtree query had never once executed with rows in it. t/shares.t is
    necessarily a set of regexes over the source -- there is no live
    Postgres in the unit suite -- and a regex cannot see a runtime
    context bug. The new guard is therefore a lint over the whole
    library, not an assertion about this one function.

