homelab-search (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Files another account has shared with you are searchable. A folder
    share brings its whole subtree, as drive intends.
  * RESOLVED LIVE, NEVER COPIED. The settled pattern here is to carry a
    drive fact across at index time, as `searchable` does. That does not
    survive this case: a carried fact lags by one indexer pass, and for a
    REVOKED share the lag is the window in which an un-shared file is
    still findable by someone else. Every request reads drive.shares, so
    revocation takes effect on the next search with no indexer pass.
  * Drive has no identity-based access rule to mirror - every
    authenticated route there is `WHERE user_email = ?` and none consults
    drive.shares; cross-account access is bearer-of-token on the
    unauthenticated /s/:token routes. So this invents the rule, and is
    deliberately STRICTER than drive: it is what drive's own notification
    email already promises the recipient.
  * A PUBLIC LINK IS NEVER SEARCHABLE. One table holds both kinds of
    share, told apart only by shared_with_email being NULL, and there is
    a live public link in the system right now. That clause has its own
    assertion.
  * Two questions, not one wider question: the ordinary account-scoped
    search, then one pass per owner scoped to THAT owner with an explicit
    allowlist of the file ids the share resolves to. Row-level security,
    all five policies and every composite foreign key are untouched, and a
    borrowed photo's text, people and tags resolve correctly because they
    are read inside the account that owns them.
  * The allowlist is the boundary, and it is a fence: search_drive refuses
    an empty one rather than running unrestricted inside someone else's
    account, and refuses a non-numeric id.
  * The owner's Private flag still wins - for the grantee too, including
    when a borrowed id is resolved directly rather than through a search.
  * The AI answer cache gains a share fingerprint. Watermarks are
    max(drive_item_id) and do not move when a share is granted or revoked,
    so without it a revoked share would go on being described by a cached
    answer for the rest of the TTL (migration 014).
  * New index on (account_id, drive_file_id): a share is expressed in
    drive file ids and that column had no index at all (migration 013).
  * Results say "Shared by <owner>"; a "Shared with me" control in
    Files-only includes (default), excludes, or shows only those; the
    count line and the exact-type picker both account for them, so an
    account owning three text files and holding seven thousand shared
    photos is still offered a way to ask for a photo.
  * Needs GRANT SELECT ON drive.shares TO homelab_search_runtime - added
    to the orchestrator with its own verify probe.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 14:08:03 -0600

homelab-search (0.2.44) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The disclosure is "Specs" and now holds the dimensions and the
    orientation alongside the camera row. The split is how a file was
    RECORDED against what it is OF - so Taken and the coordinates stay
    outside, where somebody scanning the row can see them.
  * Video gets the same treatment: codec, frame rate and bitrate join the
    specs, since a codec is the same kind of fact as an ISO and leaving
    them out while a video's dimensions went in would read as an
    oversight. Duration stays out; how long it runs is content.
  * NEW: "All raw metadata" per file, at /drive/:id/metadata. Everything
    the file carries, grouped by where it lives - EXIF, XMP, IPTC,
    MakerNotes, QuickTime atoms, the ZIP directory - because the same
    fact often appears in several of those, sometimes disagreeing, and
    that disagreement is usually why somebody opened the page.
  * This is not the hard problem it sounds like: ExifTool already is the
    one reader across every format and embedding mechanism, and it is
    already the dependency producing the indexed fields. Asking it for
    every group instead of a chosen few is the same call with the group
    kept. Measured over real files: a DJI JPEG gives 161 tags in 8 groups,
    an MP4 98, a ZIP 83, a PNG 29, a text file 15 - each in well under a
    second.
  * Same ownership path as serving the bytes: resolved through Query under
    RLS, opened only for a uuid that query just returned, never
    enumerated. Same caps as extraction - the wall-clock alarm, binary
    left as ExifTool's own summary so an embedded preview never enters
    the page, a per-value clip and a 600-tag ceiling. Every value is
    somebody else's text and the template escapes it; nothing on the page
    is built from a value.
  * Duplicates are kept here, unlike indexing: a tag written twice with
    two different values is exactly what this view is for.

